---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'AI-Era Defense Overview', href: '/docs/ai-defense' },
  { title: 'Allowlisting', href: '/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting' },
  { title: 'Attack-Surface Hardening', href: '/docs/ai-defense/hardening' },
  { title: 'Deception as a 0-Day Sensor', href: '/docs/ai-defense/deception' },
  { title: 'Capture to Virtual Patch', href: '/docs/ai-defense/response', active: true },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'loop', text: 'The Defender’s Loop', level: 2 },
  { id: 'triage', text: '1. Triage the Capture', level: 2 },
  { id: 'diff', text: '2. Diff the Pool (Proposed)', level: 2 },
  { id: 'rule', text: '3. Write the Virtual Patch', level: 2 },
  { id: 'rollout', text: '4. Roll Out and Retire', level: 2 },
  { id: 'nomodel', text: 'Why No Defender-Side Model Is Needed', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Capture to Virtual Patch" description="Turn an exploit caught in a decoy pool into an explainable, tested rule that protects production long before a CVE is published" section="AI-Era Defense" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    The attacker's model found the bug. The defender doesn't need to find it again. They need to recognise the
    exploit once it shows up, and the decoy has just shown it to them. This page is the playbook from "something
    new happened in a pool" to "production is protected", using the project's existing rule workflow.
  </p>

  <h2 id="loop">The Defender’s Loop</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={4}>
    <DocCard title="Capture" icon="crosshair" tag="Minutes 0–5" description="A diverted session sends a request no seeded CVE or known pattern explains." meta="pool + SIEM" />
    <DocCard title="Understand" icon="search" tag="Minutes 5–30" description="Read the request sequence, then see what changed in the pool." meta="Kibana timeline" />
    <DocCard title="Patch virtually" icon="code" tag="Minutes 30–60" description="A narrow Lua pattern and Suricata signature, each with positive and negative tests." meta="pure rules" />
    <DocCard title="Retire" icon="check" tag="Days later" description="When the vendor ships a fix, update through the pools and keep or drop the rule." meta="WordPress Update" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="triage">1. Triage the Capture</h2>
  <p>Start from sessions that were diverted <em>without</em> a <code>cve_pattern_match</code> reason. Those are
  the ones the rule set didn't already explain.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>In <a href="/docs/observability/kibana">Kibana</a>, filter honeypot-routed events by reason
    (<code>multiple_admin_attempts</code>, <code>rapid_automation_detected</code>,
    <code>suspicious_file_upload</code>, cumulative suspicion) and sort by sophistication class, with
    <code>ai_assisted</code> first.</li>
    <li>In the dashboard, <strong>Adversary Sessions</strong> and <strong>Forensics Analysis</strong> show the
    full request sequence for a session. Look for the request where the attacker stops enumerating and starts
    sending a structured payload to one specific plugin path or action.</li>
    <li>A later honeytoken hit from the same session or device signature confirms the exploit <em>worked</em>
    against the decoy, which is strong evidence of a real, possibly undisclosed vulnerability in the plugin
    involved.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="diff">2. Diff the Pool (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>
    Every pool starts as a known clone of production. That makes it possible to compute <em>exactly</em> what an
    exploit changed, which is the most direct evidence of what a new bug can do. The proposal adds a step to pool
    retirement: before a used pool is destroyed, the pool manager records a diff against its seed and ships it
    to the SIEM:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filesystem</strong>: new or modified files under <code>wp-content/</code> (webshells, modified
    plugin files, <code>.htaccess</code> changes).</li>
    <li><strong>Database</strong>: new admin users, changed <code>wp_options</code> (<code>siteurl</code>,
    <code>active_plugins</code>, cron hooks), injected posts or scripts.</li>
    <li><strong>Outbound attempts</strong>: connections the pool tried to open. With
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/hardening#containment">egress deny</a> these fail, but they are logged, revealing
    the attacker's second-stage infrastructure without ever contacting it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A pool diff answers "was this exploit real, and what does it give the attacker?" in seconds, with no
  reverse engineering and no model.</p>

  <h2 id="rule">3. Write the Virtual Patch</h2>
  <p>
    A virtual patch is a rule at the edge that diverts the exploit's request shape until the real fix is
    installed. PressHaven's detection is plain, explainable Lua, so this follows the existing
    <a href="/docs/detection/cves#adding">Adding a CVE</a> workflow even though there's no CVE ID yet. Use a local
    identifier and keep it narrow:
  </p>
  <pre><code>-- vulnerability_rules.lua: virtual patch for a captured, undisclosed bug
-- PH-2026-0007: unauthenticated option update via example-plugin AJAX action
-- captured in pool 3, session 9f2c…; vendor notified; remove after v2.4.1
if string.find(combined, "action=example_plugin_save_settings", 1, true) and
   not is_authenticated_admin then
    return &#123;
        cve = "PH-2026-0007",
        severity = "CRITICAL",
        description = "example-plugin unauthenticated settings update (virtual patch)",
        plugin = "example-plugin",
        attack_vector = "missing_capability_check"
    &#125;
end</code></pre>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Match the bug, not the payload.</strong> Key on the vulnerable endpoint or action plus the
    precondition (unauthenticated, wrong parameter type). An AI attacker will mutate the payload but can't avoid
    the code path.</li>
    <li><strong>Test both directions.</strong> Add a positive test with the captured request and a negative test
    with a legitimate request to the same endpoint, then run the plain-Lua suite (see
    <a href="/docs/testing">Testing</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Add the network view.</strong> Where the payload has a stable body shape, add a Suricata
    signature in the local SID range.</li>
    <li><strong>Regenerate the catalogue</strong> so the dashboard's Pattern Database and these docs list the
    virtual patch. Every active one stays visible.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Because a match diverts rather than blocks, a slightly over-broad virtual patch costs little: a legitimate
    user who trips it lands on a believable decoy rather than an error. That lets operators ship protection in
    under an hour, instead of waiting until they're sure no legitimate request could ever match.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rollout">4. Roll Out and Retire</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Notify the vendor</strong> through their normal security contact or the WordPress plugin team,
    with the captured request. This is coordinated disclosure of something already being exploited, not
    vulnerability hunting.</li>
    <li><strong>Optionally share the indicator.</strong> The pattern catalogue's export can publish the rule for
    other PressHaven deployments. Because rules are plain data, one capture can protect many shops.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply the fix through the pools first.</strong> When a release ships, use the
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/hardening#updates">pool-first update path</a>, and confirm the captured exploit
    no longer works by replaying it against the patched pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Retire or keep.</strong> Once production is patched, either remove the virtual patch or keep it
    as a detection-only signal. Clients still sending that exploit are worth diverting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="nomodel">Why No Defender-Side Model Is Needed</h2>
  <p>
    The asymmetry that makes AI-found bugs frightening, where finding is cheap and defending is expensive, only
    holds if defenders must find the same bugs first. This loop never asks them to:
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Step</th><th>What it needs</th><th>Needs a frontier model?</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Know what the shop legitimately serves</td><td>Your own traffic</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notice something outside it</td><td>Allowlist plus behaviour rules</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Watch the exploit run safely</td><td>An isolated decoy pool</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Understand what it does</td><td>Request timeline plus pool diff</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stop it reaching production</td><td>A narrow, tested Lua rule</td><td>No</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The attacker's AI does the expensive part, finding the bug. The defender only has to recognise its exploit
    once it arrives, which is cheap, auditable work that stays under the company's control. An operator who
    wants to use an LLM to help <em>read</em> a captured request or draft a rule can. Every output is then a
    readable rule with tests, not an opaque model decision in the request path.
  </p>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Protection in under an hour from first capture, before any advisory exists.
  Every virtual patch is explainable, tested and visible in the catalogue. It reuses the shipped rule, test and
  update workflows.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> The loop only starts once an exploit is diverted and noticed. An exploit that
  goes straight to production on its first try is outside it. Pool diffs and indicator sharing are proposals.
  Writing a good virtual patch still needs an operator who can read a request and a little Lua. Virtual patches
  accumulate and must be retired deliberately, or the rule set grows stale.</p>
</DocsLayout>
